"Shows the tradeoff between precision and recall across decision thresholds for a binary scoring classifier.\n\nUse when: Positive cases are uncommon and false discoveries matter. Readers need to compare retrieval or detection performance across thresholds.\n\nAvoid when: Evaluation prevalence differs from deployment without explanation or only hard labels are available.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes recall among actual positives. Vertical position encodes precision among predicted positives, with prevalence as a useful reference.\n\nBuild: Define the positive class and collect held-out continuous scores. Compute precision and recall at distinct thresholds with an explicit endpoint convention. Draw the threshold curve and label the prevalence baseline and relevant operating point.\n\nCheck: Check positive-class prevalence and evaluation sample size are stated. Verify average precision is not silently replaced by a different interpolation-area calculation. Confirm no-positives or no-predictions cases have documented handling.\n\nPitfalls: Precision changes with prevalence, so curves from different populations are not automatically comparable. A smooth visual interpolation may imply unattainable operating points; preserve the actual threshold calculations.\n\nExample: Assess a synthetic rare-defect detector, showing held-out precision versus recall and the background defect prevalence.\n\nAlternatives: ROC curve, Calibration curve, Fan chart."
